          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do European and US bodies differ on when to start medication for stage 1 hypertension?",
          "answer": "**Yes, they differ, mainly on where the blood pressure line sits and how low-risk stage 1 is handled.**\n\n**Where they differ**\n- **Diagnostic threshold.** The retrieved guidance says 130/80 mm Hg is the US threshold for stage 1. European guidance starts office hypertension at 140/90 mm Hg and calls 130 to 139 / 80 to 89 \"high-normal\" rather than stage 1. The same reading gets a different label depending on which guidance you use, and far more people count as hypertensive under the US threshold. (Source: *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*.)\n- **Treatment of low-risk stage 1.** The sources say the bodies differ on how aggressively to treat it. Because European guidance starts at 140/90, much of the population that US guidance calls stage 1 is advised on lifestyle changes alone. (Source: *Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line*.)\n\n**What the retrieved guidance says about starting medication**\nThe document describes a risk-based approach:\n- Stage 1 with established cardiovascular disease, diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or an estimated 10-year cardiovascular risk of 10% or more: drug treatment alongside lifestyle measures.\n- Stage 1 without these features: lifestyle measures first, with reassessment after three to six months before drug treatment is considered.\n\nThe passages do not say explicitly which body each of these rules comes from, so I can't attribute them to a specific US or European authority.\n\n**Where they agree**\nThe retrieved passages show agreement only in general terms. Lifestyle measures (sodium reduction, diet, activity, weight, alcohol, smoking) apply to everyone, and drug treatment is added according to risk. I found no explicit statement of full agreement on the starting threshold.\n\n**Evidence strength**\nThe retrieved passages do not give evidence grades or levels for either position, so I can't say how strong the evidence is for either approach. To check this, look up the original US and European hypertension guideline documents, which state recommendation classes and evidence levels.\n\n**Follow-up**\nOnce treatment starts or changes, review roughly monthly until the target is reached, then every three to six months once readings are stable.\n\nWhether medication is right at stage 1 depends on your own risk profile, so discuss it with a clinician who can check your readings and risk factors.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
